    "['type [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\nneighbor 10.1.1.2 ebgp-multihop 255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, you first enter BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (100 in this case)\\n2. The neighbor command establishes the BGP session (equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"neighbor\" under the group)\\n3. \"ebgp-multihop\" is needed because Cisco by default requires directly connected neighbors for eBGP, while Juniper allows this by default with \"type external\"\\n4. The multihop value 255 is the maximum allowed TTL', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.1.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\nneighbor 10.1.1.2 ebgp-multihop 255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, you first enter BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (100 in this case)\\n2. The neighbor command establishes the BGP session (equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"neighbor\" under the group)\\n3. \"ebgp-multihop\" is needed because Cisco by default requires directly connected neighbors for eBGP, while Juniper allows this by default with \"type external\"\\n4. The multihop value 255 is the maximum allowed TTL', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.1.1.2 remote-as 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.1.1.2 ebgp-multihop 255', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 